Verify this single_responsibility code in javascript.

Task: The current `Book` class is responsible for both managing book properties and handling file persistence. This violates the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP), stating a class should have only one reason to change. Your task is to refactor the `Book` class into two separate classes: `Book` (for book data) and `BookPersistenceManager` (for saving/loading book data).

Submitted code:
```
class Book {
    // 1. Efficient Instantiation: Use object destructuring in the constructor.
    // This allows you to pass a parsed JSON object directly into the class.
    constructor({ title, author, content }) {
        this.title = title;
        this.author = author;
        this.content = content;
    }

    get info() {
        return `${this.title} by ${this.author}`;
    }
}

class BookPersistenceManager {
    static saveToFile(book, filename) {
        // Fast-fail check to prevent wasted execution cycles on bad data
        if (!(book instanceof Book)) throw new TypeError("Expected a Book instance");

        // Native JSON.stringify is highly optimized at the engine level. 
        // No custom toJSON() mapping is needed.
        console.log(`Saving to ${filename}:\n`, JSON.stringify(book));
        return true; 
    }

    static loadFromFile(filename) {
        console.log(`Loading from ${filename}...`);

        // Simulated I/O. In reality, this would be: JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(filename))
        const parsedData = {
            title: "The JavaScript Way",
            author: "Baptiste Wicht",
            content: "A comprehensive guide to JavaScript."
        };

        // 2. O(1) Rehydration: Pass the raw parsed object directly to the constructor
        return new Book(parsedData);
    }
}

// --- Usage ---

// Pass a single configuration object
const myBook = new Book({
    title: "Refactoring", 
    author: "Martin Fowler", 
    content: "Improving the design of existing code."
});

BookPersistenceManager.saveToFile(myBook, "refactoring.json");

const loadedBook = BookPersistenceManager.loadFromFile("refactoring.json");
console.log(`Loaded Book: ${loadedBook.info}`);
```

Does this code correctly implement the single_responsibility concept? Check for proper OOP usage.

